<main>
<article>
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; Men's Hanfu</p>
      <h1>Men's Hanfu: Classic Chinese Menswear with Timeless Dignity</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
        Men's Hanfu emphasizes straight lines, darker colors, and a dignified presence rather than decorative flair. The key pieces -- daopao, yuanlingpao, and zhiju -- project an air of cultivated confidence that works as well for modern formal occasions as they did in imperial China.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <div class="media-card" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem; grid-template-columns: 38% 62%;">
        <div class="media-card-img">
          <img src="/images/pexels-hanfu-man-park.jpg" srcset="/images/opt/pexels-hanfu-man-park-400w.webp 400w, /images/opt/pexels-hanfu-man-park-600w.webp 600w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 420px" alt="Man in traditional Hanfu holding a folding fan in a peaceful park" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy">
        </div>
        <div class="media-card-body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Look</p>
          <h3 style="font-size:1.375rem;">Robes, sashes, and a fan — men's Hanfu is understated.</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9375rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:0.75rem;">Men's Hanfu favors the cross-collar robe and wide sash over layered gowns. A folding fan adds the finishing touch to the scholar-warrior ideal.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Key Men's Hanfu Garments</h2>
      <div class="grid-2" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;">
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Daopao (Scholar's Robe)</h3>
          <p>The most iconic men's Hanfu: a full-length cross-collar robe with wide sleeves, secured by a row of fabric ties along the side. The Ming version with its clean lines is the most popular today. Worn by scholars, officials, and educated men.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Yuanlingpao (Round-Collar Robe)</h3>
          <p>A formal round-collar robe that fastens at the front, popular in the Tang and Ming dynasties. Tang versions are bolder in color; Ming versions are more restrained. The round collar gives a polished, official appearance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Zhiju (Straight-Hem Robe)</h3>
          <p>A simpler full-length robe with a straight hem, cross-collar, and moderate-width sleeves. The everyday alternative to the formal daopao. Works well for casual Hanfu meetups and daily wear.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Tang Hufu (Riding-Style)</h3>
          <p>A Tang Dynasty ensemble: tight-sleeved jacket, trousers, and boots, influenced by Central Asian riding attire. The boldest and most practical men's Hanfu option. Works for martial arts demonstrations and active events.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Men's Hanfu by Occasion</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <thead><tr><th>Occasion</th><th>Recommended Style</th><th>Key Pieces</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Daily / Casual</strong></td><td>Zhiju or simplified Ming daopao</td><td>Cotton robe, simple sash, cloth shoes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Tea Ceremony / Cultural</strong></td><td>Song-style daopao or zhiju</td><td>Muted colors, fabric sash, folding fan</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Festival / Celebration</strong></td><td>Tang yuanlingpao or Ming daopao</td><td>Rich colors, optional rank badges, leather belt</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Wedding (Groom)</strong></td><td>Red Tang yuanlingpao or Ming daopao</td><td>Red with gold dragon embroidery, black gauze hat, matching bride</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Photoshoot / Cosplay</strong></td><td>Tang hufu or dramatic Ming daopao</td><td>Bold colors, wide sleeves, leather boots, ornamented belt</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Couple Matching Sets</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Matching couple Hanfu sets are increasingly popular for weddings, photoshoots, and festivals. The most common approach: both partners wear the same dynasty style (usually Tang or Ming) with complementary color schemes -- the bride in red with gold, the groom in darker red, blue, or black with matching gold embroidery. Many sellers offer couple packages at a slight discount compared to buying separately.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Men's Hanfu Price Ranges</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Price (USD)</th><th>What to Expect</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Starter</strong></td><td>$50 -- $100</td><td>Basic cotton or polyester robe, simple construction</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Quality Daily</strong></td><td>$100 -- $250</td><td>Cotton/linen/silk-blend, good cut, subtle embroidery or trim</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Formal</strong></td><td>$250 -- $500</td><td>Full silk or premium blends, embroidery, layered with belts and hats</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bridal / Luxury</strong></td><td>$500 -- $2,000+</td><td>Custom-made, hand-embroidered gold thread, artisan quality</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Recommended Sellers</h2>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
        <li><strong>Hanfu Story:</strong> Carries a curated men's selection with consistent sizing.</li>
        <li><strong>NewMoonDance:</strong> Good Ming-style men's daopao options.</li>
        <li><strong>Nuwa Hanfu:</strong> Premium custom men's Hanfu and couple sets.</li>
        <li><strong>Taobao:</strong> Much larger men's selection than international platforms. Search "nanshi hanfu." Popular shops: Qifengge, Jiming.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">How to Experience Men’s Hanfu Culture in China</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">Men’s Hanfu focuses on cultural practice – tea ceremony, calligraphy, music, and martial arts. These cities offer the richest men’s Hanfu experiences.</p>
      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Nanjing – Scholar Spirit and Ming Atmosphere</h3>
        <p><strong>Why here:</strong> Nanjing, the early Ming capital, is the spiritual home of men’s Hanfu. The Ming Xiaoling Sacred Way, Zhan Garden, and Fuzimiao provide perfect backdrops for men’s daopao. Several men’s specialty shops around Fuzimiao focus on historically accurate Ming styles. Tea houses welcome Hanfu patrons for traditional tea ceremonies.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Transportation:</strong> Nanjing Lukou Airport or high-speed rail from Shanghai (1 hour). Metro Line 1 to Sanshanjie for Fuzimiao. Line 2 to Muxuyuan for Ming Xiaoling.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Where to stay:</strong> Fuzimiao / Qinhuai River (300-600 RMB/night). Xinjiekou city center (350-800 RMB/night). InterContinental in Zifeng Tower (900-1,800 RMB/night).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Local food:</strong> Yanshui ya (salted duck, 30-50 RMB), yaxue fensi tang (duck blood soup, 15-25 RMB), tangbao (giant soup dumplings, 20-35 RMB) near Fuzimiao. Nanjing Impressions chain (60-100 RMB/person).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Hanfu activities:</strong> Men’s Hanfu shops on Jiangnan Gongyuan street. Traditional tea ceremony along the Qinhuai River. Ming Xiaoling Sacred Way at sunrise for magnificent photography.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Xi’an – Martial Spirit and Tang Grandeur</h3>
        <p><strong>Why here:</strong> Xi’an’s men’s Hanfu leans toward Tang Dynasty – bold and martial. Men’s round-collar robes and hufu riding-style ensembles are popular. Walk or cycle the 14 km Ancient City Wall in Hanfu. Several martial arts schools offer Hanfu-friendly traditional archery and sword-form experiences.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Transportation:</strong> Fly into Xi’an Xianyang Airport. Metro Line 2 to Yongningmen (City Wall). Taxis: 10-30 RMB.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Where to stay:</strong> Ancient City Wall courtyard hotels (400-900 RMB/night). Bell Tower/Drum Tower area (300-700 RMB/night). Budget hostels near South Gate (80-150 RMB/night).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Local food:</strong> Yangrou paomo (30-50 RMB), biangbiang noodles (20-35 RMB), lamb skewers (5-10 RMB). Defachang dumpling banquet (80-150 RMB/person).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Hanfu activities:</strong> Cycle the City Wall in Hanfu (bike rental 45 RMB/3 hrs). Traditional archery near Dayan Pagoda (200-400 RMB for 1-2 hours). Men’s Tang yuanlingpao rental near South Gate.</p>
      </div>
